> > dusa....@gmail.com schrieb am Freitag, 7. Juli 2023 um 22:21:40 UTC+2:
> > > Dear Anna,
> > >
> > > It would likely be easier to follow with a minimal reproducible example (I find myself better at reading the R code behind the question).
> > > Logical remainders do not have OUT = 0 in the truth table, but "?". True however, when remainders are excluded, they are indeed allocated a value of 0 in the truth table.
> > >
> > > Did you use the argument "exclude" in the function truthTable(), or did you use the function esa() from package SetMethods?
> > > I would recommend the first method, although I'm sure the function esa() is well tested.
> > >
> > > Excluded remainders should not appear in the simplifying assumptions, that's for sure. That is why a reproducible example would make your question much easier to answer (I need to see this happening in order to assess what exactly is going on).
> > >
> > > All the best,
> > > Adrian
> > >
> > > On Friday, July 7, 2023 at 1:45:16 PM UTC+3  madame...@gmail.com wrote:
> > > > Dear QCA community, dear Adrian, I am currently working on a fsQCA in R using the QCA and the SetMethods packages and I encountered repeatedly an issue with the Enhanced Standard Analysis: Despite excluding contradictory assumptions for the most parsimonious solution and the intermediate solution, the excluded logical remainders (with OUT = 0 in the esa truth table) seem to be included in the enhanced parsimonious and the enhanced intermediate solution as simplifying assumptions or easy counterfactuals (at least they show up when I use "sol_y_pesa$SA" or "sol_ny_i$i.sol$C1P1$EC"). I don't quite understand this - shouldn't these logical remainders be excluded from the minimization in ESA?
